[{:db/id "q-321074/field-1/26a9d8be180c405ed7098f5c509504feebbe58fdbdc0ec6d37421971e7d2700e" :answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321074/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9e7a06-a7ad-5fea-bd64-541162aae1b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321074/field-1/26a9d8be180c405ed7098f5c509504feebbe58fdbdc0ec6d37421971e7d2700e" :answer-field/choices ["q-321074/field-1/26a9d8be180c405ed7098f5c509504feebbe58fdbdc0ec6d37421971e7d2700e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321074" :question/id #uuid "01251fc0-f57a-5dfd-8833-fe87964205c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-321074/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321074"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-320871/field-1/1fde10792b31696e8c1e01ece5b3e83eae9d9ade6726671ee9051e02581c622f" :answer/id #uuid "1c7fc5ff-31b3-5e16-9851-ec767b6d80bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x^2+4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320871/field-1/1c2e38ece7a881f9fb993812030bfd64908c73c8a21fb4fec7f2ffe7883f3d05" :answer/id #uuid "05c3766d-30d2-536f-92b5-c3bb14d03783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x^2-4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320871/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1ccaa9-f0ed-5847-8879-af06dd7d1c30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320871/field-1/1fde10792b31696e8c1e01ece5b3e83eae9d9ade6726671ee9051e02581c622f" :answer-field/choices ["q-320871/field-1/1fde10792b31696e8c1e01ece5b3e83eae9d9ade6726671ee9051e02581c622f" "q-320871/field-1/1c2e38ece7a881f9fb993812030bfd64908c73c8a21fb4fec7f2ffe7883f3d05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320871" :question/id #uuid "fc90ad49-5a43-5603-ac83-3120ed37c3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-320871" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-320871/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320871"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-15678"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-6828/selection/120c6bd8c260c91b96f66034c7c4d80023c5d817e7cec72c073dc3b826e94b03" :answer/id #uuid "ab2cdb10-b33a-5a4b-8829-e0d571cc75f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6828/selection/a0d382e0a48420b0e2b2749e864efa1e3ebb4c1349be23790b92b46fd1b42af7" :answer/id #uuid "818ee6df-487f-54dc-8aad-ac170036e72a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6828/selection/9d13654e9057185ea0f630a388482dc6fd33e4d00f847b55c9c3d047efdb0ff8" :answer/id #uuid "befb103f-f7d7-5b97-bce7-b0c6058bf32d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6828/selection/187c8433fbdd03a5f4153b254db548e3f3b1c266a9154d49ac64a4f58450aa99" :answer/id #uuid "f92eecc0-fe92-51ca-a4c0-c3fca69528f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6828/selection/2c7f9efb0c0d6e2730dac06bd62d046d69b268593867248e22bfc53195ec4cb7" :answer/id #uuid "6bcb4c4e-4b0c-51f8-896d-bbc8c716d0ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x+5},\\,x\\ne -5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}