:answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321141/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "88d37378-5483-53f8-8d90-61fa3cee1f14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321141/field-1/98d0f708029fa7c943231183b808a809d1de48636b3f258fa22b65fa0e1850a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-321141/field-1/98d0f708029fa7c943231183b808a809d1de48636b3f258fa22b65fa0e1850a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321141/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2686efd1-0658-53d1-ab66-14ea0dd151d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321141/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5c5077-c40d-5cca-8f37-702494277cc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321141/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-321141/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321141" :question/id #uuid "67774c28-5664-5512-8ad3-78b93096bff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-321141/field-1" "q-321141/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321141"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-15751"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-15752"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection/e160064eac7876ff37ea559dae2d2bf80288f61f65a4989bb221254e01c25cf6" :answer/id #uuid "ed3965cb-daa0-571c-ad5a-7b1e6b2a3cae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+{x}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection/653c9f6df3ff2834bdadb24d41c7d50b926774f68228108aa397286bb41eb47a" :answer/id #uuid "e2ec2db6-cfc3-5c6b-b7b6-67c8d8b29b77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}+(x+3)^{2}=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection/d3082d99b8d5bc578e71d84a063b00bed591808c9da3004f3f4fe2387b5b9190" :answer/id #uuid "006f8b63-3556-56db-adbc-896b2ae222b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}+(x-3)^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection/4a3a7ae220d63bfbb61966d8d5a8bfeaf5cf82f4026c8ce49b6345adc1fb1dc0" :answer/id #uuid "77164973-aa24-527c-a317-f8d784f5434a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection/a4fc0aebbfaed2caebdcd2edfa8d7fed75ec4a005d23497bcdae9629465ec1e7" :answer/id #uuid "da8025c4-36c6-5909-a799-b25ebd05ecc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "191f1b4c-768e-5f06-b2e7-4d5b537e182d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15679/selection/4a3a7ae220d63bfbb61966d8d5a8bfeaf5cf82f4026c8ce49b6345adc1fb1dc0" :answer-field/choices ["q-15679/selection/e160064eac7876ff37ea559dae2d2bf80288f61f65a4989bb221254e01c25cf6" "q-15679/selection/653c9f6df3ff2834bdadb24d41c7d50b926774f68228108aa397286bb41eb47a" "q-15679/selection/d3082d99b8d5bc578e71d84a063b00bed591808c9da3004f3f4fe2387b5b9190" "q-15679/selection/4a3a7ae220d63bfbb61966d8d5a8bfeaf5cf82f4026c8ce49b6345adc1fb1dc0" "q-15679/selection/a4fc0aebbfaed2caebdcd2edfa8d7fed75ec4a005d23497bcdae9629465ec1e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15679" :question/id #uuid "e929f172-a4de-58f2-bf84-26e1ce65ea1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15679" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15679/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15679"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-102682/selection/047111be595c952efdff62721b35a7098eb00e3a844c6f8c3f997ac8569f3da7" :answer/id #uuid "66417d04-d38c-5e39-9e51-1e8879351c79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102682/selection/bf98d8ddeeaf2ce38b265239da2156c1f1a2f0bc67b8f85883ea7524b9d28832" :answer/id #uuid "64d67936-1aa5-5a94-9279-d04853ab073a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102682/selection/ccf8c7596315187001b3de1643ed0fe481087a9f1c853a1287220e03c19336c9" :answer/id #uuid "7f10cc1e-080c-5d