,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-102682/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection/6914e8eecbf86b3eed1ff652b1553311bf17c68b0ec811a35d9b5b39176c8a2a" :answer/id #uuid "c634e6c3-cda2-5dfb-a33d-c1e865c60fe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection/b02a7faeecbfcd200e707231e58f11f846707e63b8c9b508dc53ce68eafa1cae" :answer/id #uuid "b19e8739-b4ac-5000-9a8b-b9e06712287f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection/af4af10fa76864dd276f2946e1d62a52ffb26f19d9eb35849e93af98b0a741e9" :answer/id #uuid "8de4e91b-d865-55a8-a215-bb5ad9542c59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection/c1edc20d2e5ee72e77fdd36bd2cdc7fb70731de579fcede9f62cb38d736dcedd" :answer/id #uuid "55c47b5a-ac8f-5c23-be2a-727e9abcdd19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-3{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection/d82a336eb87bc06f54c6dc4323b5ac3c5cad3554cdd339216462bfbac1ec5aea" :answer/id #uuid "b31d0db4-2412-5c7e-a81b-24094e7b2a01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0523683b-dd86-5d70-801b-158ffe2a1306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15761/selection/af4af10fa76864dd276f2946e1d62a52ffb26f19d9eb35849e93af98b0a741e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-15761/selection/6914e8eecbf86b3eed1ff652b1553311bf17c68b0ec811a35d9b5b39176c8a2a" "q-15761/selection/b02a7faeecbfcd200e707231e58f11f846707e63b8c9b508dc53ce68eafa1cae" "q-15761/selection/af4af10fa76864dd276f2946e1d62a52ffb26f19d9eb35849e93af98b0a741e9" "q-15761/selection/c1edc20d2e5ee72e77fdd36bd2cdc7fb70731de579fcede9f62cb38d736dcedd" "q-15761/selection/d82a336eb87bc06f54c6dc4323b5ac3c5cad3554cdd339216462bfbac1ec5aea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15761" :question/id #uuid "7ce3b0b3-27e3-5f38-ad49-c4188b0daf7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15761" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15761/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5087"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5101"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
